# DEEP REFERENCE — Architecture, Math, Communication, Sensors

> Audience: AI agents and humans who need to modify this system safely. This
> section is written to be self-contained. Read the relevant subsection before
> touching code. For the running list of *known bugs*, see
> [`BallAlgo/CODE_REVIEW_FINDINGS.md`](../../CODE_REVIEW_FINDINGS.md) — do not
> duplicate that content here; this file documents how the system is *meant* to
> work.

## 0. What this robot is and the one-paragraph mental model

This is a **RoboCup Junior Soccer (Open League)** robot. Two robots per team play
autonomous soccer with an orange ball on a ~182 cm × 243 cm field with a blue
goal on one short wall and a yellow goal on the other. Each robot is a **two-brain
system**: a **Raspberry Pi CM5 (BallAlgo)** is the "cortex" — it does all vision,
LiDAR localization, state estimation, strategy, and motion *planning* — and a
**Teensy 4.1 (Offense2026)** is the "cerebellum/spinal cord" — it owns the motors
and real-time sensors and *executes* the plans. The Pi computes **where to be and
how to get there** and streams time-stamped velocity/acceleration "action chunks"
over UART; the Teensy rotates them into the body frame and drives the wheels.

**Canonical branches (June 2026):** Pi = whatever is checked out in `BallAlgo/`;
Teensy = **`FinalDraft1`** in `Offense2026/`. The older `lineControl` /
`NewPathAlgoTest` branches the code review referenced are obsolete.

**Recent architecture decisions (this is the current intent — honor it):**
- **The Pi plans BOTH offense and defense.** It arbitrates role and emits the
  appropriate action chunks. The Teensy is **role-agnostic**: it executes whatever
  chunks arrive. Switching a robot's role mid-match is a pure Pi-side decision.
- The legacy Teensy-side **orbit/dampen ball-chase and the `Cam` ASCII parser were
  removed.** The camera and all vision math live **only on the Pi**.
- The Pi→Teensy link is a **single binary framed protocol**; the legacy ASCII
  perception path was removed. Exactly one object drains the Pi UART on the Teensy
  (`TrajectoryExecutor::processSerial`).

