## 13. Issues found during this documentation pass (NOT already in CODE_REVIEW_FINDINGS.md)

These were noticed while writing this doc; verify and, if confirmed, add to the
findings file. Confidence noted per item.

- **N1 — Redundant BNO055 I²C polling per loop (latency + intra-loop heading skew). [High confidence]**
  `CompassSensor::getOrientation()` and `getOmegaRadS()` each issue a fresh
  `bno.getEvent(...)` I²C transaction, and they are called multiple times per
  control loop from different places (`loop()` → `setRobotHeadingDegrees`;
  `processSerial → sendTelemetry`; `execute()`; etc.). That's ~3–5 I²C reads per
  iteration. Two harms: (a) several ms of I²C latency added to every loop —
  directly relevant to trajectory timing/quantization; (b) the heading used for
  chunk rotation, telemetry, and line-PID are sampled at *different instants*, so
  they disagree slightly within one iteration. **Fix:** read heading + omega once
  at the top of `loop()`, cache, and have everyone use the cached values.

- **N2 — LiDAR deskewer is fed mouse velocity even when it is stale. [Medium confidence]**
  `MotionPipeline::predictStep` always calls `deskewer_.addMotionSample(..., odo.mouseVxBodyMmS, odo.mouseVyBodyMmS, odo.omegaRadS)` regardless of `odo.mouseFresh`. When the mouse telemetry is stale, the EKF correctly falls back to a plain `predict()`, but the **deskewer still ingests the stale/zero velocity**, so scans get de-smeared with the wrong motion during telemetry dropouts. **Fix:** gate the motion sample on `odo.mouseFresh`, or pass the EKF's own velocity estimate.

- **N3 — ActionChunk wire format has no layout/version guard (unlike telemetry). [Medium confidence]**
  Telemetry is protected by a byte-identical `static_assert` on both sides. The
  **highest-rate, most safety-critical** message — the action chunk — is hand-packed
  on the Pi (`packActionChunk`) and `memcpy`'d into the Teensy `ActionChunk` struct
  with only a `pkt_len >= 32` sanity check. Any field-order/padding/size drift between
  the repos silently corrupts motion commands. (H6 found one concrete instance — the
  kick/dribbler bytes — but the underlying *absence of a structural guard* is the
  broader risk.) **Fix:** define a shared packed `GlobalAction`/chunk-header layout
  with a `static_assert` and a `schemaVersion`/expected-size byte checked on receipt.

- **N4 — Look-ahead blend can self-defeat because the chunk start is stamped in the future. [Low–medium confidence; refines M3]**
  `recordChunk` stores the chunk's start pose as the *look-ahead* pose and stamps
  `startTimePi = now + latency`. On the next iteration `predictChunkState(nowPi)`
  sees `nowPi < startTimePi`, returns `tElapsed = 0`, and yields the future start
  pose; the tracking error `e = |EKF_now − future_start|` is then systematically
  inflated, pushing `α→0` so the planner largely ignores the chunk prediction it
  was supposed to blend with. Worth instrumenting `α` to confirm. **Fix:** compare
  the EKF against the chunk state evaluated at a matching timestamp.

- **N5 — `runRobot()` line-avoidance still commands a heading hold. [Low confidence / behavioral]**
  During a line event the Teensy calls `movement.movement(avoidanceAngle, lineAvoidanceSpeed, 0, false)`, which (via `findCorrectionRelZero(0)`) simultaneously tries to rotate the robot back to the *zeroed* heading while escaping the line. That heading correction can fight the escape translation. Confirm this is intended; if not, pass the current heading as the target so it translates straight off the line.

